{
  "lesson_summary": "Today's lesson plans to teach students to recognise the place value of each digit in 2-digit numbers (tens, ones/units) and to count objects and numbers up to 99, forwards and backwards. The lesson will use pictures of tens circles and loose counters to represent 2-digit numbers, with an example of 6 tens and 1 one making 61. It will also cover counting on a number strip, including stepping forward from 68 and stepping back from 70.",
  "lesson_summary_short": "Today's lesson plans to teach students to recognise the place value of digits in 2-digit numbers, using pictures of tens circles and loose counters for 61.",
  "checks_summary": "This quiz checks whether the class can identify tens and ones in 2-digit numbers, read numbers from place value pictures, and count forwards and backwards on a number strip.",
  "questions": [
    {
      "slo_id": "S4",
      "level": "recall",
      "question": "What does one loose counter mean?",
      "options": [
        "one one",
        "one ten",
        "one hundred"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "A loose counter represents a single unit, which is one 'one'.",
      "selected_because": "The lesson defines what a loose counter represents.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Confuses loose counters with tens circles",
        "2": "Overestimates the value of a loose counter"
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "That's right! A loose counter means one 'one', representing a single unit.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Not quite. A loose counter represents one 'one', while a circle of 10 counters means one 'ten'.",
          "2": "No, a loose counter means one 'one'. One hundred is a much larger value."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null,
      "source_quote": "One circle of 10 counters means one ten; a loose counter means one one.",
      "teaching_error": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S3",
      "level": "understand",
      "question": "What number do these show?",
      "options": [
        "24",
        "6",
        "42"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "The picture shows 2 tens circles, which is 20, and 4 loose counters, which is 4 ones. So, 20 + 4 = 24.",
      "selected_because": "The lesson uses a picture of 24 to highlight a common misconception.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Counts every mark as one, ignoring the value of tens circles",
        "2": "Reverses the order of tens and ones digits"
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "Excellent! The picture shows 2 tens and 4 ones, which makes the number 24.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Careful! Remember that each circle has 10 counters inside. You need to count the tens circles first, then the loose counters. The picture shows 2 tens and 4 ones, which is 24.",
          "2": "Not quite. Remember to write the tens digit first, then the ones digit. The picture shows 2 tens and 4 ones, so the number is 24."
        }
      },
      "figure": {
        "type": "count_objects",
        "rows": [
          {
            "picto": "counter",
            "count": 10,
            "group": 10,
            "color": "accent"
          },
          {
            "picto": "counter",
            "count": 10,
            "group": 10,
            "color": "accent"
          },
          {
            "picto": "counter",
            "count": 4,
            "color": "ink"
          }
        ]
      },
      "figure_role": "read_off",
      "source_quote": "Children may count every mark as one, for example, reading a picture representing 24 as 6 marks.",
      "teaching_error": {
        "said": "reading a picture representing 24 as 6 marks",
        "correct": "The picture represents 24"
      }
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S1",
      "level": "understand",
      "question": "In the number 61, what is the value of the digit '6'?",
      "options": [
        "sixty",
        "six",
        "sixty-one"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "In 2-digit numbers, the first digit from the left represents the tens place. So, '6' in 61 means 6 tens, which is sixty.",
      "selected_because": "The lesson uses 61 as a worked example to explain place value.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Confuses the digit with its place value",
        "2": "Confuses the digit's place value with the whole number"
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "That's correct! The '6' in 61 is in the tens place, so its value is sixty.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Not quite. 'Six' is the digit itself, but its value in 61 is sixty because it's in the tens place.",
          "2": "No, sixty-one is the whole number. The '6' in 61 represents sixty, because it's in the tens place."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null,
      "source_quote": "Count tens circles: 6, so 6 tens = 60",
      "teaching_error": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S2",
      "level": "understand",
      "question": "What number is 2 steps forward from 68?",
      "options": [
        "70",
        "69",
        "66"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "Starting from 68, one step forward is 69, and two steps forward is 70.",
      "selected_because": "The lesson includes an example of stepping forward twice from 68.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Counts only one step forward",
        "2": "Counts backwards instead of forwards"
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "You got it! Two steps forward from 68 means 68, then 69, then 70.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Almost! 69 is one step forward from 68. You need to take two steps forward, which leads to 70.",
          "2": "Careful! The question asks for steps forward. Stepping forward from 68 means counting up, so 68, 69, 70."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null,
      "source_quote": "On the strip, Hira steps forward twice from 68",
      "teaching_error": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S5",
      "level": "understand",
      "question": "How do you write 5 tens and 6 ones?",
      "options": [
        "56",
        "65",
        "506"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "To write a 2-digit number, you write the tens digit first, then the ones digit. So, 5 tens and 6 ones is 56.",
      "selected_because": "The lesson ends with an example of writing a number from tens and ones.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Reverses the order of tens and ones digits",
        "2": "Combines the tens value and ones digit incorrectly"
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "That's right! You write the tens digit first, then the ones digit, so 5 tens and 6 ones is 56.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Not quite. Remember to write the tens digit first, then the ones digit. So, 5 tens and 6 ones is 56.",
          "2": "No, 5 tens and 6 ones means 50 + 6, which is 56. You write the tens digit first, then the ones digit."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null,
      "source_quote": "Check by naming values: 5 tens and 6 ones is 50 and 6, which is 56.",
      "teaching_error": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S2",
      "level": "understand",
      "question": "What number is 1 step back from 70?",
      "options": [
        "69",
        "71",
        "60"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "Stepping back one from 70 means counting down by one, which gives 69.",
      "selected_because": "The lesson includes an example of stepping back once from 70.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Counts forwards instead of backwards",
        "2": "Subtracts too much or confuses with tens place"
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "Correct! One step back from 70 is 69.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Careful! The question asks for one step back, which means counting down. One step back from 70 is 69.",
          "2": "Not quite. One step back from 70 means subtracting 1, which gives 69."
        }
      },
      "figure": null,
      "figure_role": null,
      "source_quote": "Hamza steps back once from 70.",
      "teaching_error": null
    },
    {
      "slo_id": "S3",
      "level": "understand",
      "question": "What number does the picture show?",
      "options": [
        "61",
        "16",
        "7"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "The picture shows 6 tens circles and 1 loose counter. 6 tens is 60, and 1 one is 1. So, 60 + 1 = 61.",
      "selected_because": "The lesson uses this exact picture as a worked example.",
      "distractor_misconceptions": {
        "1": "Reverses the order of tens and ones",
        "2": "Counts total number of marks, ignoring place value"
      },
      "option_feedback": {
        "correct": "That's right! The picture shows 6 tens and 1 one, which is 61.",
        "wrong": {
          "1": "Not quite. Remember to read the tens circles first, then the loose counters. The picture shows 6 tens and 1 one, making 61.",
          "2": "Careful! Each circle has 10 counters. You need to count the tens circles and the loose counters separately. The picture shows 6 tens and 1 one, which is 61."
        }
      },
      "figure": {
